<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian II of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilian II was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1848, when his father Ludwig I abdicated, until his death in 1864. He accepted liberal reforms after the revolution of 1848, and in German affairs he tried to keep Bavaria independent between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. A patron of learning, he invited scholars from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and founded the Historical Commission of the Bavarian Academy of Sciences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Maximilian II of Bavaria" width="320" height="538" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Albert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert,_Joseph_-_K%C3%B6nig_Maximilian_II._von_Bayern_(1811-1864)_(Zeno_Fotografie).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maximilian was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 28 November 1811, the eldest child of Crown Prince Ludwig, later King Ludwig I, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therese-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Therese of Saxe-Hildburghausen</a>, and was named after his grandfather, King Maximilian I. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. His father brought him into the council of state in 1836. He showed a studious disposition and once said that, had he not been born a prince, he would have chosen to be a professor. At the chateau of Hohenschwangau near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a>, which he had rebuilt, he gathered artists and scholars around him. In 1842 he married the Prussian princess Marie Friederike Franziska Hedwig, and they had two sons, Ludwig II and Otto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ludwig I's abdication on 20 March 1848 called him suddenly to the throne, and his choice of ministers promised a liberal regime. An uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarian-palatinate">Bavarian Palatinate</a> in 1849 was put down with the help of the Prussian army. From 1850 his government leaned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, though he steered between classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, Prussian-inspired Pan-Germanism and the Ultramontanes. He attended the Frankfurt Assembly but opposed its plans, refused to recognise its imperial constitution and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> restore the federal diet.</p><p>In 1850 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and their allies moved troops towards Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> backed down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punctation-of-olmutz">Punctation of Olmütz</a>, which strengthened the alliance of Bavaria with Austria. He nonetheless tried to keep Bavaria independent by playing the two powers against each other. In 1863 he supported Austria's reform plan at the Frankfurt Fürstentag.</p><p>He invited scholars such as Geibel, Liebig, Heyse and Sybel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> whatever their religion, which angered conservative Catholics and Protestants. He also financed studies of the art, costumes, dialects and customs of the Bavarian peasantry, with the help of his secretary Franz Xaver von Schönwerth, to support a separate Bavarian identity against Pan-Germanism. He consulted ministers and experts before deciding, which caused delays, as did his frequent journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Constant ill health often kept him abroad or in the countryside. After a brief illness he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 10 March 1864 and was buried in the Theatinerkirche. His son Ludwig II succeeded him. He commissioned Maximilianstrasse and the Bavarian National Museum in Munich and founded the Maximilian Order for Science and Art in 1853. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-christian-andersen">Hans Christian Andersen</a>, who visited him, described him as young and highly amiable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maximilian II of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maximilian II of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
